<Layout title="The Celdon Shooper Principle — Wahidul Alam Nahid" active="dispatches">

  <header class="article-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="meta">QA · Sept 21, 2025</div>
      <h1>The Celdon Shooper Principle: Why Edge Cases Aren't Optional</h1>
      <div class="novel-banner">
        <img src="/images/blog/celdon-shooper.jpg" alt="A tester reacting wide-eyed to something on a laptop screen" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="article-body">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p>Some testers chase bugs. Celdon Shooper hunts realities most teams don't even imagine. His stories remind us that what looks like an "edge case" is often just overlooked prioritization.</p>

      <blockquote>"That's not an edge case. That's just lazy prioritization." — Celdon Shooper</blockquote>

      <p>🧠 His keyboard has hotkeys for <em>assertion</em>, <em>sarcasm</em>, and <em>screenshot+eye-roll</em>.</p>

      <p>☕ He logs bugs with titles like:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>💻 "Critical: UI betrays user in obscure yet statistically possible scenario."</li>
        <li>📊 His test plan includes intergalactic timezone offsets—just in case.</li>
        <li>🛠️ He once rejected a deployment because the animation "did not spark joy."</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Last week, Celdon was testing a flow no one else even knew existed. A user with regional settings from a country not even in the drop-down, a language combo only he could enable, and a specific series of clicks that made the "Next" button disappear into another dimension.</p>

      <p>Celdon calmly said: "I got a situation." The room froze. When a colleague whispered "That's such an edge case…" he stood, sipped peppermint tea, and replied:</p>

      <blockquote>"If your product only works when it's treated perfectly, it doesn't work."</blockquote>

      <p>QA isn't about covering the 80%. It's about that Celdon sense—where logic, paranoia, and empathy unite to protect the people no one thought to build for.</p>

      <p><strong>So here's to the edge-case hunters, the path-breakers, the Celdon Shoopers of the world.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="back-link wrap"><a class="btn-ghost" href="/#dispatches">← All dispatches</a></div>

</Layout>
